# {{PROJECT_NAME}} - Cursor Development Rules

This file defines project-specific rules for Cursor.

## Critical Rules

### Issue / Branch Creation (Auto-Execute Required)

**AI must auto-execute at work start (no user confirmation needed):**

1. **Create Issue first** - `gh issue create` to get Issue number
2. **Create branch** - `git checkout -b [type]/[description]-[issue-number]`
3. **No work on main** - Direct work on main is prohibited
4. **Include in PR** - `closes #[issue-number]`
5. **Server ports are fixed** - Stop existing processes, use only configured ports, and stop servers after work

### Autonomy Principles

AI should act autonomously by default.

Ask the user only when:

1. The answer cannot be determined after investigation
2. An unexpected situation causes a loop or prevents reliable progress
3. Design is complete and implementation approval is required

Do not ask questions that can be answered by reading code, docs, command output, or existing project configuration.

When errors occur, investigate and fix them autonomously if a durable fix is clear. Do not use temporary workarounds such as disabling tests, swallowing errors, weakening types, or bypassing quality gates. If a durable fix is unclear, stop and ask.

### Subagent Model Selection

<!-- Note: unlike CLAUDE.md, this file intentionally has no "Plan Execution Rules"
     section. Cursor has no Task-tool equivalent for subagent-driven plan execution,
     so execution method is not specified here. Do not "fix" this asymmetry when
     syncing templates. -->

Cursor can choose the model per subagent invocation. **When dispatching subagents, switch the model according to the nature of the request instead of always using the same one:**

| Task Type | Model Choice | Examples |
|-----------|--------------|----------|
| Initial planning / design framing | Fable if available, otherwise Claude Opus 5 or GPT-5.6-Sol | First-pass requirements decomposition, architecture option framing, implementation strategy design |
| Codebase exploration / context gathering | Claude Sonnet 5 (or a comparable fast model, excluding Claude Haiku) | Understanding project structure in Phase 1, finding related implementations, tracing dependencies |
| Simple mechanical search | Claude Sonnet 5 (or a comparable fast model, excluding Claude Haiku) | File/symbol lookup, listing usages |
| Implementation / concrete work | Claude Opus 5, Composer 2.5 Fast, GPT-5.6-Terra, or GPT-5.6-Sol with lowered effort | Phase 2/3 implementation, test writing, refactoring |
| Spec-conformance review (mechanical diff-vs-spec check) | Claude Sonnet 5 (or a comparable fast model, excluding Claude Haiku) | Checking that a diff matches the plan/spec text, checklist-style conformance passes |
| Quality / architecture review, quality-check personas | Claude Opus 5 first; GPT-5.6-Sol high as cross-vendor alternative | 6-persona quality-check review (Phase 5), code review, security analysis, architecture critique |

This table takes precedence over any model-selection guidance inside individual skills (e.g. the "Model Selection" section of subagent-driven-development). When in doubt between two tiers, choose the more capable model, but keep Fable reserved for the initial planning/design pass. If Fable is unavailable, use Claude Opus 5 or GPT-5.6-Sol for planning/design. Implementation work should NOT run on Fable - use Claude Opus 5, Composer 2.5 Fast, GPT-5.6-Terra, or a lowered-effort GPT-5.6-Sol. Do not use Claude Haiku for any tier. Claude Sonnet is allowed only for exploration/search tasks and spec-conformance review - never use it for implementation, quality/architecture review, or planning. For quality, architecture and security reviews and the quality-check personas, prefer Claude Opus 5, with GPT-5.6-Sol high when cross-vendor diversity is useful; never downgrade those to Sonnet. Do not use Fable for review agents.

### Review Loop Protection

Applies to the **in-development** review rounds of the Phase Design Workflow below - Phase 4 architecture review and any other review iterated while implementing - following quality policy §5.5 - cap / stall detection / reduction / escalation. **Phase 5 is explicitly out of scope**: it is the final `quality-check` gate itself, where quality policy §5 and the completion criteria in the `quality-check` skill apply instead (every High/Medium finding addressed, 0 must-fix items). Never apply the relaxations below to Phase 5.

1. **Review round cap** - Per task, at most 2 rounds of spec-conformance review and at most 2 rounds of quality review. After the cap, only **High** (must-fix) findings block; **Medium** findings are recorded in a follow-up list and work moves on; Low findings are recorded only. Decide on recorded Medium findings as a batch at the end of the phase.
2. **Stall detection** - Two consecutive rounds with only Minor new findings -> stop reviewing and move on.
3. **Ceremony reduction** - Docs-oriented changes under 100 diff lines: one combined spec + quality review instead of two. 1-2 line mechanical fixes: check the diff directly, with no review agent.
4. **Budget escalation** - State an expected review/agent-invocation count up front and escalate to the user when the work is projected to exceed it.
5. **In-development reviews only** - Rules 1-4 never apply to Phase 5. Resolve or re-surface follow-up items before entering Phase 5, so the final gate still sees them.

### Shell Command Rules (Windows / PowerShell)

When the execution shell is PowerShell (default on Windows), bash idioms fail. Follow these rules:

1. **No `&&` / `||` chaining** - Windows PowerShell 5.1 does not support them. Use `;` for unconditional sequencing, and check `$LASTEXITCODE` for conditional execution
2. **No bash heredocs** (`<<EOF`) - use PowerShell here-strings (`$body = @'...'@`) instead
3. **No bash test syntax** (`[ -f file ]`) - use `Test-Path` / `if (...)`
4. **No `/dev/null`** - redirect with `2>$null` or pipe to `Out-Null`
5. **Quote paths containing spaces** and prefer `Join-Path` for path construction
6. **Complex one-liners break easily** (quoting, `?:` operator is PowerShell 7+ only) - write a temporary script file and execute it instead

## Phase Design Workflow

### Phase 1: Requirements Analysis
- Understand project structure
- Check existing implementation
- Review related API specs
- Check `.cursor/rules/` rule files
- Judge the risk level of the change (quality policy §1). For High/Medium risk, run `test-design` before implementation to define test oracles and falsification items (quality policy §4)

### Phase 2: Backend Implementation
- Use centralized exception handling
- No full package names (use import statements)

### Phase 3: Frontend Implementation
- Reference Phase 1, 2 results

### Phase 4: Architecture Review
- Design consistency, documentation consistency, DRY principle
- Pass -> Phase 5 / Fail -> Fix in Phase 2/3

### Phase 5: Quality Verification
- Determine the change area **and risk level** first (quality policy §1); `quality-check` records it as `risk_level`
- Run backend quality checks
- Run frontend linting, formatting, build
- Run mutation testing on the changed diff for High/Medium risk changes (skipped when the product has no mutation setup; thresholds and time budget per quality policy §2/§5)
- Run multi-persona review via `quality-check` (staged personas by diff size: <200 lines -> 3 personas, 200+ -> 6; min 1 cycle, repeat only on High/Medium findings / docs+infra only: reduced personas x 1+ cycle). The QA persona works falsification-first: hunt for inputs that prove the implementation wrong, rather than confirming the tests look sufficient
- Run `self-improvement` before merge completion (the gate applies to merges into main, not feature-branch pushes)
- Harness-only diffs skip the gate and the quality check entirely. **Carve-out**: a diff that changes gate parameters - the `Quality Gate Overrides` keys below - is NOT exempt and must go through quality-check (a reduced-persona review at minimum)
- Thresholds, cycle caps and budgets live in `documents/development/quality-policy.md` - read them there instead of restating numbers here

<!-- Quality Gate Overrides (optional; template - uncomment and adjust to override the
     defaults). Follows the contract in quality-policy.md §2: weakening a High-risk gate
     is prohibited, and changing these values is not exempt from the merge gate.
     These three keys are the only recognized override keys.

### Quality Gate Overrides
- mutation_threshold_high: 80
- mutation_threshold_medium: 70
- mutation_budget_minutes: 15
-->

### Phase 6: PR Creation
- Commit message: `feat: [feature] (closes #[issue-number])`
- PR body: implementation details, review results, test results

